{"title": "Programmable Cochlear Interfaces | Berkay Özbek", "children": [{"tag": "section", "attrs": {"class": "project-hero container"}, "children": [{"tag": "a", "attrs": {"class": "back", "href": "/#research"}, "children": ["← Research portfolio"]}, {"tag": "p", "attrs": {"class": "eyebrow"}, "children": ["MSC RESEARCH / IMPLANTABLE ACOUSTIC INTERFACES"]}, {"tag": "h1", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Programmable circuits for an electronic cochlea."]}, {"tag": "p", "attrs": {"class": "lead"}, "children": ["Low-power analog signal processing for fully implantable cochlear interfaces, from a measured nanopower filter to a complete 12-channel front-end design."]}, {"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "project-meta"}, "children": [{"tag": "span", "attrs": {}, "children": ["METU · 2019–2021"]}, {"tag": "span", "attrs": {}, "children": ["TSMC 0.18 µm HV BCD CMOS"]}, {"tag": "span", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Supervisor: Prof. Haluk Külah"]}]}, {"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "actions"}, "children": [{"tag": "a", "attrs": {"class": "text-link", "href": "https://doi.org/10.1109/BioCAS54905.2022.9948682", "target": "_blank", "rel": "noopener"}, "children": ["Analog front-end paper ", {"tag": "span", "attrs": {"aria-hidden": "true"}, "children": ["↗"]}]}, {"tag": "a", "attrs": {"class": "text-link", "href": "/documents/Berkay_Ozbek_MSc_Thesis.pdf", "target": "_blank", "rel": "noopener"}, "children": ["Read the MSc thesis ", {"tag": "span", "attrs": {"aria-hidden": "true"}, "children": ["↗"]}]}]}]}, {"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "container project-content"}, "children": [{"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "metric-band"}, "children": [{"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "metric"}, "children": [{"tag": "strong", "attrs": {}, "children": ["12 channels"]}, {"tag": "span", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Programmable analog front-end"]}, {"tag": "small", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Full system validated in simulation"]}]}, {"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "metric"}, "children": [{"tag": "strong", "attrs": {}, "children": ["9.03 µW"]}, {"tag": "span", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Default AFE power"]}, {"tag": "small", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Simulation · all channels active"]}]}, {"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "metric"}, "children": [{"tag": "strong", "attrs": {}, "children": ["13.2 nW"]}, {"tag": "span", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Standalone filter power"]}, {"tag": "small", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Measured · 950 Hz · 1 V"]}]}]}, {"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "story-intro"}, "children": [{"tag": "h2", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Signal conditioning for implantable acoustic sensors"]}, {"tag": "div", "attrs": {}, "children": [{"tag": "p", "attrs": {}, "children": ["An implantable acoustic sensor can replace the external microphone, but its small electrical output needs low-noise amplification and frequency-selective processing. My MSc research develops the analog interface between a non-resonant piezoelectric acoustic sensor and the stimulation-side electronics."]}, {"tag": "p", "attrs": {}, "children": ["A low-noise amplifier feeds twelve bandpass channels and variable-gain amplifiers. Digital configuration adjusts the frequency, bandwidth and gain distribution so the electronics can accommodate different sensor responses and channel requirements."]}]}]}, {"tag": "figure", "attrs": {"class": "research-figure "}, "children": [{"tag": "a", "attrs": {"href": "/assets/cochlear-architecture.webp", "class": "figure-open", "data-caption": "The proposed acoustic signal-processing chain", "aria-label": "Enlarge figure: The proposed acoustic signal-processing chain"}, "children": [{"tag": "img", "attrs": {"class": "", "src": "/assets/cochlear-architecture.webp", "width": "1208", "height": "664", "alt": "The proposed acoustic signal-processing chain", "loading": "lazy", "decoding": "async"}, "children": []}, {"tag": "span", "attrs": {"class": "enlarge"}, "children": ["Enlarge ↗"]}]}, {"tag": "figcaption", "attrs": {}, "children": [{"tag": "strong", "attrs": {}, "children": ["The proposed acoustic signal-processing chain"]}, {"tag": "p", "attrs": {}, "children": ["The analog front-end amplifies, separates and scales the sensor signal. The stimulation-side blocks provide system context; the thesis develops the front-end circuits."]}, {"tag": "a", "attrs": {"class": "source", "href": "/documents/Berkay_Ozbek_MSc_Thesis.pdf#page=23", "target": "_blank", "rel": "noopener"}, "children": ["MSc thesis · Fig. 1.5 · p. 5 ", {"tag": "span", "attrs": {"aria-hidden": "true"}, "children": ["↗"]}]}]}]}, "\n", {"tag": "section", "attrs": {"class": "content-section"}, "children": [{"tag": "p", "attrs": {"class": "eyebrow"}, "children": ["FIRST CONTRIBUTION / MEASURED FILTER"]}, {"tag": "h2", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Measured nanowatt gm-C bandpass filter"]}, {"tag": "p", "attrs": {"class": "prose"}, "children": ["A second-order Gm-C filter uses bulk-driven, subthreshold folded-cascode OTAs to realise small transconductances. Bias control and capacitance adjustment tune the centre frequency and quality factor."]}, {"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "figure-pair"}, "children": [{"tag": "figure", "attrs": {"class": "research-figure "}, "children": [{"tag": "a", "attrs": {"href": "/assets/filter-silicon.webp", "class": "figure-open", "data-caption": "The fabricated filter and its test PCB", "aria-label": "Enlarge figure: The fabricated filter and its test PCB"}, "children": [{"tag": "img", "attrs": {"class": "", "src": "/assets/filter-silicon.webp", "width": "1198", "height": "456", "alt": "The fabricated filter and its test PCB", "loading": "lazy", "decoding": "async"}, "children": []}, {"tag": "span", "attrs": {"class": "enlarge"}, "children": ["Enlarge ↗"]}]}, {"tag": "figcaption", "attrs": {}, "children": [{"tag": "strong", "attrs": {}, "children": ["The fabricated filter and its test PCB"]}, {"tag": "p", "attrs": {}, "children": ["The standalone filter occupies 0.085 mm² active area. Measurements use a 1 V filter supply and a separately powered 1.8 V observation buffer."]}, {"tag": "a", "attrs": {"class": "source", "href": "/documents/Berkay_Ozbek_MSc_Thesis.pdf#page=64", "target": "_blank", "rel": "noopener"}, "children": ["MSc thesis · Fig. 2.26 · p. 46 ", {"tag": "span", "attrs": {"aria-hidden": "true"}, "children": ["↗"]}]}]}]}, {"tag": "figure", "attrs": {"class": "research-figure "}, "children": [{"tag": "a", "attrs": {"href": "/assets/filter-tuning.webp", "class": "figure-open", "data-caption": "Measured frequency programmability", "aria-label": "Enlarge figure: Measured frequency programmability"}, "children": [{"tag": "img", "attrs": {"class": "", "src": "/assets/filter-tuning.webp", "width": "700", "height": "525", "alt": "Measured frequency programmability", "loading": "lazy", "decoding": "async"}, "children": []}, {"tag": "span", "attrs": {"class": "enlarge"}, "children": ["Enlarge ↗"]}]}, {"tag": "figcaption", "attrs": {}, "children": [{"tag": "strong", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Measured frequency programmability"]}, {"tag": "p", "attrs": {}, "children": ["The fabricated filter demonstrates tunable acoustic-band responses. These measurements belong to the standalone first-generation filter."]}, {"tag": "a", "attrs": {"class": "source", "href": "/documents/Berkay_Ozbek_MSc_Thesis.pdf#page=65", "target": "_blank", "rel": "noopener"}, "children": ["MSc thesis · Fig. 2.27 · p. 47 ", {"tag": "span", "attrs": {"aria-hidden": "true"}, "children": ["↗"]}]}]}]}]}, "\n", {"tag": "div", "attrs": {"class": "table-wrap", "tabindex": "0", "role": "region", "aria-label": "Standalone filter: experimental results"}, "children": [{"tag": "table", "attrs": {}, "children": [{"tag": "caption", "attrs": {}, "children": ["Standalone filter: experimental results"]}, {"tag": "thead", "attrs": {}, "children": [{"tag": "tr", "attrs": {}, "children": [{"tag": "th", "attrs": {"scope": "col"}, "children": ["Parameter"]}, {"tag": "th", "attrs": {"scope": "col"}, "children": ["Measured result"]}]}]}, {